    "result_text": "I implemented `build_claude` in `app/claude.py`, and all tests pass (`pytest -q`: 2 passed).\n\n**You can't pass the existing `httpx` client to Claude.** The pinned `anthropic==1.8.0` is built on `httpx2`, not `httpx`. It rejects `httpx` objects, including the `httpx.Timeout`, the client, and transports. So `build_claude` creates its own `httpx2` client using the SDK's `DefaultHttpxClient`, which keeps the SDK's connection defaults. Nothing new was installed, because `httpx2` already comes with `anthropic`.\n\n**What's reused from `app/http.py`:**\n- **Header:** `EGRESS_TAG` sets the `X-Egress-Tag` header.\n- **Logging:** `log_egress` runs as the request hook, so every attempt, retries included, gets one line on `app.egress`.\n- **Timeouts:** `CONNECT_TIMEOUT` and `READ_TIMEOUT` (5s / 60s) are used to build an `httpx2.Timeout`. This one timeout line is the only part not shared directly, since `app.http.TIMEOUT` is an `httpx` object. I also pass the timeout to `Anthropic(...)` so the SDK's per-request timeout matches.\n- **Retries:** `max_retries=2`.\n- **Transport:** a `transport` you pass in is handed to the HTTP client. When it's `None`, it's left out so the SDK sets up its normal default transport.\n\n`app/http.py` and `fetch_status` are unchanged, and their existing test still passes.\n\nI added `tests/test_claude.py`. It uses an `httpx2.MockTransport` whose first reply is a 529 error, so the client retries once. The test checks that both attempts carry the header and are each logged once, that the timeouts are 5s connect / 60s read, and that `max_retries` is 2.\n\n**One catch for your stub transport:** if it's an `httpx.MockTransport`, the SDK will refuse it. It needs to be an `httpx2.MockTransport` (or another `httpx2` transport)."
  },
